1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,100
Geochemistry Plotting Tools
Classify, plot and explore geochemistry inside QGIS

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,500
Making the most of your geochemical data
A: classification · B: population statistics · C: interactive exploration

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:10,700
A · Classification of the samples
From field lithologies to TAS classes

4
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:15,150
We start with 149 samples, coloured by field lithology
Category LITHNAME, labels SAMPLEID; all samples selected (red)

5
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:19,750
Wilson (1989) TAS diagram, ready to read
One of nine built-in discrimination diagrams

6
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:23,950
Push the plot style back to the layer
The previous layer style is saved first

7
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:26,950
The map now matches the plot
Same colours, markers and categories

8
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:31,350
Write the TAS classification to the attribute table
119 of 149 samples get a TAS name (Wilsn89TAS); 30 fall outside all fields

9
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:33,950
Re-classify the layer on the new TAS field
Unclassified (NULL) samples are switched off from here on

10
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:36,950
Samples mapped by TAS class
A categorised style on Wilsn89TAS, in seconds

11
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:39,100
B · Population statistics
Granite, granodiorite and diorite only

12
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:42,150
Keep only granite, granodiorite and diorite
58 samples selected by expression on Wilsn89TAS

13
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:47,150
REE spider diagram, chondrite-normalised
Colours taken straight from the layer symbology

14
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:49,750
Statistics overlays: mean ± 2σ
Individual samples fade into the background

15
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:52,350
Interquartile range bands
Compare populations at a glance

16
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:55,350
Min–max envelopes with mean ± 2σ
Clean figures, ready for a report or a talk

17
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:57,500
C · Interactive exploration
Bubble plots, ternaries and two-way selection

18
00:00:57,750 --> 00:01:03,750
Fe₂O₃T vs MgO, bubbles scaled by SiO₂
Linear scaling, with a size legend on the plot

19
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:07,950
The same samples on a Na₂O–MgO–Fe₂O₃T ternary
Custom apexes, same SiO₂ bubble sizing

20
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:10,950
Freehand selection directly on the plot
Here: the most Na₂O-rich granites

21
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:13,550
Selected samples are labelled
Labels from SAMPLEID; copy the table in one click

22
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:16,350
…and selected on the map at the same time
12 samples, shown in red

23
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:20,150
Save named selections with notes
Recall them later from the Selections list

24
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,300
Everything in one QGIS panel
Diagrams, statistics, selection, symbology sync and export

